@page "/changelog"
@layout PublicLayout

<PageTitle>Change Log — Nornis</PageTitle>

<article class="nornis-public-page">
    <div class="nornis-eyebrow">Change Log</div>
    <h1>What's new</h1>
    <Stonemark Kind="StonemarkKind.Rule" />

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.24 — November 9, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Your settings, sorted too.</em> Your settings now has three tabs: This world (your display name, your characters and claiming a player, all for the world you have open), AI (your keys, and which model each kind of work runs on) and Account (your sign-ins, Bibliotaph and light or dark). The tab you're on is in the address.</li>
            <li><em>Links land on the right tab.</em> "Add your own key" opens AI; reconnecting Bibliotaph, or coming back from linking a sign-in, opens Account.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.23 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Settings, sorted.</em> A world's Settings page now has five tabs: World (name, description, game system and key art side by side), Public page (the public link and the public Ask budget), AI and costs (the daily and Prep budgets above what has been spent), Processing (whether summary updates go through review, then the queue) and Export and delete. The tab you're on is in the address, so a link opens it.</li>
            <li><em>Save what you see.</em> Each tab has its own Save, and it saves only that tab. What you type on one tab is still there when you come back from another.</li>
            <li><em>Invite links moved to Members,</em> under the people at the table, where you'd look for them. Only the GM sees them.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.22 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>A welcome worth stopping for.</em> The welcome page opens on a picture of the demo world's coast, with one button: sign in and explore a ready-made world. Its example and a short "your first ten minutes" are drawn from that same campaign, so what you read is what you'll find.</li>
            <li><em>Straight into the demo world.</em> That button signs you in and opens your demo world with the guided tour, making it first if you don't have one yet. If you already have one, it simply takes you back to it.</li>
            <li><em>Demo worlds come with key art.</em> A new demo world opens Home on its picture. It's yours to replace in Settings like any other.</li>
            <li><em>Signed in already?</em> The public pages say Open Nornis rather than Sign in.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.21 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Less to review.</em> When Nornis reads a source it now judges, for each thing it would propose, what your table will do with it: core, supporting, or incidental. Incidental detail (scene dressing, passers-by, prices, routine travel, a passing mood) is dropped before it reaches your review queue. The source keeps the text, so nothing is lost to Ask or to a later re-read.</li>
            <li><em>Fewer storylines, and the right ones.</em> A storyline is now something the table is pursuing: someone intends something, and there is a moment where it could resolve. A character's nature or reputation, the world reacting to who someone is, mood, foreshadowing and one-off warnings are recorded as facts about the people concerned, not as arcs of their own. A source opens at most two new storylines, and prefers advancing one you already have.</li>
            <li><em>Closed means closed.</em> Extraction now sees which storylines you have marked Dormant or Resolved, and leaves them be: no twin, no new open questions. If a session plainly reopens one, it proposes setting it Active again and says why.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.20 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>More room to write.</em> When you edit a source, its details (type, visibility, when it happened, campaign and whether to extract from it) move into a Details card on the right, and you edit the title at the top of the page beside Save and Cancel. The notes take the rest of the page. The note about an already-processed source is one line.</li>
            <li><em>Capture matches.</em> Capture puts its title and type on one row, moves visibility, when, campaign and extraction into a Details card above the Loremaster, and puts Save draft and Save &amp; process at the top of the page.</li>
            <li><em>Link only where it matters.</em> The Link field shows only for Web Link sources, or for a source that already has one.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.19 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>The window stays still.</em> On a computer, the sidebar and the bar along the top no longer scroll away: the page beneath them scrolls on its own, footer and all, and a new page opens at its top.</li>
            <li><em>Editors scroll, not the page.</em> On Capture, and when you edit a source or a character sheet, the page fits the window: the notes editor takes the height the other fields leave and long notes scroll inside it under a toolbar that stays put, so the fields and the Save button below are always in view. Its scrollbar sits at the editor's right edge.</li>
            <li><em>Rails fit what you can see.</em> The Loremaster beside Capture and Talk it through beside Prep fit the space under the top bar, so their message box stays in view. On a phone, pages scroll as they always have.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.18 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>The adventure's name, where you see it.</em> On an adventure's page, click the title or the pitch at the top to edit it, and pick its campaign from the menu beneath. They stay above every tab, so the Adventure card on the Book tab is gone. Enter saves the title, Ctrl+Enter the pitch, and Escape puts either back.</li>
            <li><em>Prep fits the window.</em> On a wide enough screen the Prep page stays the height of the window. The draft, the section you're writing and Talk it through each scroll inside their own column, so Save, Undo and the message box are always in view. A narrow window scrolls as before.</li>
            <li><em>Typography beside Book details.</em> On the Book tab, the Typography card moves to the second column, above Book details.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.17 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Choose the booklet's type.</em> A Typography card on an adventure's Book tab sets the font, size and line spacing its booklet prints in, with a sample set the width of a booklet column. Choose from seven open-licence book faces that Nornis ships with the printer: Newsreader, EB Garamond, Literata, Crimson Pro, Source Serif 4, Lora and Alegreya. Sizes run from 8 to 14 pt and line spacing from 1.0 to 2.0; leave them be and the booklet prints as it always has, in Newsreader 10 pt at 1.3.</li>
            <li><em>What follows the type.</em> Body text, read-aloud and props print in your choice, and the cover and rights page use the same face. Headings, stat blocks and tables keep their own faces and grow or shrink with the size, so the page keeps its proportions. A print you've already filed keeps the type it was printed in.</li>
            <li><em>The Loremaster knows the type too.</em> On the Book tab it sees what the booklet is set in and which faces you can choose, so it can suggest one that suits the adventure.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.16 — November 8, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Your world, front and centre.</em> Home opens on the world's name set beside its own picture, with a line saying the game system, how many sessions you've played, how many entries the codex holds and when you last played. The Ask box sits beneath it.</li>
            <li><em>Key art.</em> A new Key art card in Settings gives the world one landscape picture. Upload your own, or describe the picture and paint two options on your own OpenAI key, then choose one and say which part to keep in view: the top, the middle or the bottom. Up to eight generated images a month, counted in the Prep budget. Everyone in the world sees it, and so does the public page when it's on, so keep secrets out of it.</li>
            <li><em>The map, when there's no art.</em> Without key art, Home shows the map your role can see on the Map page, softened behind the name. With neither, it stays typeset.</li>
            <li><em>In the sidebar and on the public page.</em> The world switcher shows the key art in place of the mountain icon, and a public world's page opens on its picture.</li>
            <li><em>Pictures.</em> On Profile, the Models card's "Cover art" row is now "Pictures": the same OpenAI image model paints covers and key art.</li>
            <li><em>Structure.</em> An adventure's Sections tab is now called Structure: the outline and the section you have open, as before.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.15 — November 7, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Colour that means something.</em> Each kind of entry has its own muted colour: slate for characters, moss for places, ochre for items, plum for factions, teal for events, old gold for storylines and slate violet for concepts. It marks the entry's icon and its kind label on Codex and Storyline cards, Home's recent changes, the Connected list beside an entry and the entry's own header, so a person, a place and a plot read apart before you read the label. The graph's dots use the same colours. Buttons and links keep the one terracotta accent.</li>
            <li><em>Prep is a shelf.</em> The Prep list shows each adventure as a book: its cover art when it has some, otherwise a typeset cover with the title, as the booklet prints it.</li>
            <li><em>The map, full width.</em> The Map page gives the map the page's whole width. The places panel sits beneath it, or on a wide screen floats over its right edge, where you can put it away; choosing a place brings it back.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.14 — November 7, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>The main action stands out.</em> The button that does a page's or a dialog's main job, such as New adventure, Capture source or Add to library, is the terracotta one again. Since 2.0 it had been a grey block that looked switched off beside the outlined buttons around it. Buttons and tabs read in sentence case rather than capitals.</li>
            <li><em>Words, not identifiers.</em> Sources, entries and Home say "GM only", "located in" and "part of" where they used to print the names Nornis uses inside.</li>
            <li><em>Quieter lists.</em> A card or a source only shows its status when there is something to notice: an entry that is dormant, resolved or archived, a source that is queued or failed. Party visible sources no longer say so on every row.</li>
            <li><em>Titles that match the menu.</em> Storylines opens on the storyline cards and Timeline on the chart, each titled to match; Map is titled Map, and the GM's Settings page is titled Settings.</li>
            <li><em>A smaller masthead.</em> The sidebar's logo is now one line, so the Table and GM sections, Prep among them, fit on a laptop screen without scrolling the menu. In dark mode the logo is light, where it used to be black on near-black.</li>
            <li><em>Delete, one step away.</em> On an adventure, Print is the main action and Delete has moved into the ⋯ menu beside it, since deleting an adventure can't be undone.</li>
            <li><em>Empty pages with a way forward.</em> An empty Library, Prep or Review now has its next step inside the box, over a faint layered pattern.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.13 — November 7, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>An adventure in three tabs.</em> An adventure's page now has three tabs, in the order you work: Design for the whole draft, Sections for the outline and the section you have open, and Book for everything about the booklet as a book. Moving between tabs saves what you typed on the one you leave, and the tab you're on is kept in the address, so a reload or a link opens it again.</li>
            <li><em>Talk it through on the right.</em> On every tab, Talk it through is a rail on the right that stays in view as you scroll. The conversation, and anything you've typed in its box, stays put when you move between tabs. The outline is only on Sections, where it is used.</li>
            <li><em>What the Loremaster reads.</em> The table brief and the source books now sit above Talk it through, folded to one line that says what each is set to. Open it to change either. Both go into every draft and answer, and neither prints.</li>
            <li><em>The Book tab.</em> The cover art, Handouts and Documents sit beside the adventure's title, pitch and campaign and its Book details. The title, pitch and campaign are edited from the Adventure card there, which replaces the Details button in the page head.</li>
            <li><em>Talk the book through.</em> On the Book tab, the Loremaster sees the cover as it stands, its latest art brief and layout, and the handouts, rather than a section, so you can work out a cover with it: ask for a few scenes, refine one, and paste the brief you like into the Cover's brief. It keeps lettering, twists and Hidden truth off a cover, since the players see it once the book is shared.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.12 — November 6, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>The Loremaster writes in the Design draft.</em> On the Design tab, ask the Loremaster to write the adventure, bring something new in, or rework a scene, and it now makes the change in the draft itself instead of pasting the text into the conversation. Its reply stays short: what it changed, and a line saying so. Show changes lists each change before and after. All of one reply's changes are one step of Undo on the draft, so a change you don't like is one click away from gone.</li>
            <li><em>When a change doesn't fit.</em> If a change quotes a passage that isn't in the draft any more, it is listed as not applied and the rest still go in; you can still apply it by hand. Anything you typed in the draft is saved before the Loremaster starts, and the draft is read-only until it finishes, so neither of you writes over the other.</li>
            <li><em>Longer answers.</em> A Design reply can now be long enough to write a whole adventure in one go. An answer that runs out of room is caught every time, including the ones that used to stop mid-change without saying so, and nothing it half-wrote is put into the draft.</li>
            <li><em>The Loremaster remembers the draft.</em> Changes it made earlier are kept in the conversation as short notes rather than whole passages, so a long writing session no longer pushes the draft out of what the Loremaster remembers.</li>
            <li><em>Read from your question.</em> After you send a message, the conversation scrolls so your message is at the top and the reply grows below it, so a long reply reads from its start.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.11 — November 6, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Web research in Prep.</em> Talk it through has a Web research switch beside Send. Turn it on and the Loremaster may search the web before it answers that one message, through your own OpenAI, Claude or Gemini key, then the switch turns itself off again. It searches at most five times, treats what it finds as reference material, and keeps to your world's record where a page disagrees. It never opens a page of its own choosing, and it is told never to put your world's secrets into a search.</li>
            <li><em>From the web.</em> A researched answer lists the pages it drew on under its text, each opening in a new tab, or says when it cited none. Put in section, Put in draft and Apply copy the text only. Gemini's search suggestions are shown with the answer.</li>
            <li><em>Rights.</em> When a researched answer goes into an adventure, its booklet's rights page adds that research drew on public web sources with AI assistance and that the author is responsible for rights in any third-party setting, and Book details says so too. The print page reminds you once, and sharing such a print with the party reminds you again. Neither stops you.</li>
            <li><em>Cost.</em> Searches are billed by your provider per search, about a cent each, and count in the Prep budget with the rest of the turn. Costs shows how many searches a turn ran. Web research needs a key of your own; it isn't offered on the trial, on Nornis's own models, or on OpenAI-compatible endpoints.</li>
            <li><em>Enter sends.</em> In Talk it through, Enter sends your message and Shift+Enter starts a new line.</li>
            <li><em>Talk it through stays in view.</em> On a wide window, where Talk it through sits beside the section, it now stays in place as you scroll a long section, and its messages scroll on their own so the box and Send are always in reach.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.10 — November 4, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Your own AI keys.</em> Profile now has an AI keys card. Add an OpenAI, Anthropic (Claude) or Google Gemini API key, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint with the models it serves. Nornis checks a key with the provider before saving it, stores it encrypted, and never shows it again. The AI work in every world you created then runs on your key, for everyone who plays in it, and the provider bills you directly. A ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini subscription isn't an API key; the card links to where each provider makes one.</li>
            <li><em>Pick your models.</em> A Models card on Profile chooses the model for each kind of work: Ask and world tools, Reading sources, Prep, and Small jobs. The models Nornis has tested and priced come first. After them, "More from your key" lists what else your key can reach, marked as not tested by Nornis; those are costed as an estimate, and if one can't read an image or return the shape Nornis asks for, the call fails and says so rather than quietly switching models.</li>
            <li><em>When a key fails.</em> A rejected key, or an account out of credit, now says exactly that wherever you were working, with a link to the provider's billing page when it's a credit problem. Sources that failed for that reason are read again as soon as you save a key that works.</li>
            <li><em>Who paid.</em> Costs shows which calls your keys paid for and which Nornis did, and marks a model's cost as estimated when Nornis hasn't priced it.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.9 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Cover art.</em> An adventure's booklet can have a painted cover. Ask the Loremaster for an art brief or write your own, and Generate paints two options on your own OpenAI key (add it under Profile, AI keys); pick the one you like. Or upload your own art. Nornis sets the title itself, so the art never carries lettering. Choose Full bleed, Framed or Classic module for the layout. Each adventure can generate up to 12 images, and their cost counts in the Prep budget.</li>
            <li><em>A rights page.</em> Page 2 of the booklet now carries its credits: the title, Nornis Press, the author's copyright, the system and levels, the print date and the Nornis release, a sentence on how AI was used, and a notice that the booklet isn't affiliated with the game's publisher.</li>
            <li><em>AI use, recorded.</em> Book details has an "AI was used to make this adventure" box. It ticks itself and locks when the Loremaster wrote words into the adventure or painted its cover. Talking with the Loremaster, splitting, gathering and importing don't count, so an adventure you wrote yourself can still say so. You can tick it yourself at any time.</li>
            <li><em>Handouts and maps.</em> A Handouts card on the adventure picks maps and handouts from the Library, this adventure's own first, or uploads one straight into it. The booklet ends with a Handouts and maps section in your order: images on their own pages, turned to landscape when wide, and PDF handouts' pages added as they are. Choosing a handout never changes who can see it.</li>
            <li><em>Print props.</em> The print page's Print props downloads every prop box in the adventure on its own page in large type, for cutting out and handing to the players.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.8 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Design mode.</em> An adventure now has a Design tab beside Sections: one working draft of the whole adventure, in the same editor with every Prep tool. Write it, paste it in, or import Markdown files into it. It never prints and never exports.</li>
            <li><em>Work on the draft with the Loremaster.</em> In Design mode the conversation sees the whole draft. When it suggests a change, it shows the passage and its replacement as a card, and nothing changes until you press Apply. Undo on the draft steps back through every change you applied.</li>
            <li><em>Split into sections without changing a word.</em> Split cuts the draft at its own # and ## headings, or where the Loremaster suggests, and shows the plan first so you can rename, re-kind or merge sections. Nornis checks that the sections hold exactly the draft's words before anything is saved. Gather joins the sections back into the draft.</li>
            <li><em>A table brief.</em> Tell the Loremaster about your table once (the party, their level, how long you play, who to write for) and every draft and answer for that adventure uses it.</li>
            <li><em>Source books.</em> Drafting and the conversation now read the world's Library and Bibliotaph books at the GM's level and cite them by page. Choose up to five source books on an adventure, such as the module you're adapting, to have the Loremaster read only those.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.7 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Find a document by what it is.</em> Chips above the Library list show your books, maps, handouts, prints and everything else, each with its count, and the page remembers the one you chose. A search box and a Newest or Title sort sit beside them.</li>
            <li><em>Documents that belong to an adventure.</em> On a document's page you can say which adventure it belongs to, or choose one as you upload. It leaves the main shelf and shows under that adventure in the Library's Adventure menu, so one-shot maps and handouts don't bury your books. Prints stay on the shelf.</li>
            <li><em>An adventure's documents on its Prep page.</em> A Documents card lists what belongs to the adventure and its prints, with an Upload button that files a new map or handout straight to it.</li>
            <li><em>Change an upload's category</em> from its page. Books from Bibliotaph and Prep prints keep theirs.</li>
            <li>Only the GM sees where documents belong. Players see the Library as before, and nothing about who can read a document changes.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.6 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Stat blocks that look the part.</em> Start a stat block with <code># Name</code> and an italic line under it, group its entries under <code>## Actions</code> or <code>## Traits</code>, and the booklet prints a name bar, short scores side by side in one row, and each group as its own run of entries. It works for any system; Nornis never computes the numbers.</li>
            <li><em>Item cards.</em> The new <em>Item</em> button adds equipment and loot: a name, a line like "Wondrous item, rare", entries such as weight and value, and a description after a <code>---</code> line.</li>
            <li><em>GM notes.</em> The <em>GM note</em> button marks a note for you alone, like a hard exit or a fallback, printed as a shaded box.</li>
            <li><em>The Loremaster writes them too</em>, in the same form, for the adventure's own game system, edition and levels from Book details. It points to a published book's page rather than copying its stat block.</li>
            <li><em>One fact per block.</em> Export to Nornis files each named stat block or item as one fact on its character or item, and the editor warns you about a block with no name, an empty one, or one too long to file whole.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.5 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>File to Library in one click.</em> The print page files the booklet straight to the Library, dated, GM-only and against its adventure. There is no PDF to save and upload any more.</li>
            <li><em>The booklet is a PDF.</em> Nornis lays it out itself and the print page shows it in your browser's PDF viewer, where you can print it; <em>Download</em> saves it. What you see is exactly what is filed.</li>
            <li><em>Pages that read naturally.</em> Each chapter flows down the left column, then the right, then on to the next page, with no gaps between scenes. A heading never sits alone at the foot of a column, a short read-aloud box, prop or stat block stays in one piece, a long table repeats its header, and list bullets stay inside their column.</li>
            <li>A line break you type in a section is a line break in the booklet, as it is in the editor, and a checklist prints with its boxes.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.4 — November 3, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Book details.</em> Every adventure has the details a library catalogues a book by: game system, edition, levels and year, edited one at a time on the adventure's page. The game system is the world's until you set one. The type is always <em>Nornis adventure</em>, the publisher <em>Nornis Press</em>, and the author your username.</li>
            <li><em>On the cover.</em> A printed booklet shows them under the title, with the credits at the foot of the cover, and the print page is titled after the adventure, so a PDF you save is named for it.</li>
            <li><em>Into Bibliotaph as filed.</em> A booklet filed to the Library keeps its details as they were that day, and Bibliotaph receives them exactly when it catalogues the booklet, instead of guessing from the PDF.</li>
            <li><em>From your Markdown.</em> Import Markdown reads <code>system</code>, <code>edition</code>, <code>levels</code> and <code>year</code> from front matter at the top of the first file, and tells you about any key it left out.</li>
            <li>The Prep list now uses the same list as Sources and the Library, with filters for adventures exported to Nornis, published to the Library, or neither.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.3 — November 2, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>One account, several sign-ins.</em> Your Profile now lists the ways you can sign in to your account. <em>Link another sign-in</em> takes you to the sign-in screen; sign in with Discord, Google, Microsoft or any other option there and it opens the same account, with the same worlds, from then on. You can remove a sign-in you no longer use, as long as it isn't the one you're signed in with and isn't your last.</li>
            <li>A sign-in is only ever added by you, signed in, proving you hold it. Nornis never joins accounts because their email addresses match, and a sign-in that already has its own Nornis account can't be linked to another.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.2 — November 2, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Your books, from Bibliotaph.</em> Connect your Bibliotaph on your Profile and each world you run gets its own collection there. The books you put in it appear on the world's Library page with their covers, GM-only until you share one with the party, and a book that leaves the collection leaves the Library.</li>
            <li><em>Read at the table.</em> A player opens a shared book in Bibliotaph's reader, from the Library or a shelf link, for an hour at a time and without a download. The GM sees who opened what on the book's page.</li>
            <li><em>Quoted by the page it's printed on.</em> The Loremaster searches the world's Bibliotaph books and cites the printed page number. A player's question only ever searches the books shared with the party, and if Bibliotaph can't be reached the answer says it used world memory only.</li>
            <li><em>Excerpts from Bibliotaph.</em> Enrich from Library finds the closest pages of your Bibliotaph books, and Bibliotaph's <em>Send to Nornis</em> brings a page range here to confirm. Up to 12 pages become a Library excerpt and go through Review like any source. Pages of a book you keep from AI in Bibliotaph can't be filed.</li>
            <li>Bibliotaph can use Nornis as storage for a world's sourcebook uploads and Prep booklets; it reads them from Nornis and never keeps a copy. Maps and handouts stay in Nornis.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.1 — November 2, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><em>Talk it through.</em> Every adventure has a conversation with the Loremaster beside the editor, for the questions a draft doesn't answer: who else the cult could have recruited, what the clerk knows, three ways the chase could end. It knows your world, Hidden truth included, and the section you have open. The conversation is kept with the adventure and only the GM sees it. Nothing in it changes the adventure until you choose an answer and <em>Put in section</em>, adding it to the end of a section or replacing what is there, and <em>Undo</em> takes that back like a draft.</li>
            <li><em>Threads to use.</em> On an adventure's overview, the hidden truths your world ranks as most ready to reveal are listed with the reasons, the same ranking the Reveal page uses. <em>Use in this adventure</em> adds one to the instruction box, and drafting the overview sees the top five whether you use one or not.</li>
            <li>A conversation is never part of the record. It isn't extracted, Ask doesn't read it, and it never reaches the public site; it travels in the world's export with the rest of Prep, and goes when the adventure does. Its answers come out of the same monthly Prep budget as drafting, metered as their own line on the Costs tab in Admin.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 3.0 — November 2, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Prep. GMs have a new place in the sidebar for the adventure they're about to run. An adventure is an outline of sections (an overview, a session clock, chapters, scenes and a quick reference) that you write in the notes editor, reorder by dragging or with the arrow buttons, add to and cut. A new one starts from a skeleton you can ignore. One you already have in Markdown files comes in through <em>Import Markdown</em>, chapters and scenes intact. Prep is the GM's alone: players and observers never see it, and a GM viewing as a player doesn't either.</li>
            <li>The Loremaster drafts with you. On any section, <em>Draft</em> writes it and <em>Revise</em> rewrites it, with an instruction if you give one: "make it tense", "the clerk is lying". It writes from your whole record, the Hidden truth included, because the GM is the author. You watch the words arrive, can stop at any point, and nothing is saved unless the draft finishes. Every draft keeps the version it replaced, so <em>Undo</em> takes you back.</li>
            <li>Four things look different in Prep, each with its own toolbar button: <em>read-aloud</em> text, <em>props</em> (the letters and notices players get to hold), <em>questions</em> for the table, and <em>stat blocks</em> and equipment lists, written as plain label and value lines in any system's terms and never computed.</li>
            <li><em>Print</em> lays the adventure out as a two-column booklet: a cover, each chapter on a new page, read-aloud in a ruled box, props on parchment and stat blocks framed. Save it as a PDF from the print dialog and <em>File to Library</em> puts it on the GM's shelf, dated, as a handout. A booklet can hold the world's secrets, so it is filed GM-only, and making it party-visible asks first.</li>
            <li><em>Export to Nornis</em> sends a settled adventure, or one chapter of it, into the world as a GM-only source. The people, places and secrets it names come back as proposals in Review, like any session's; the scenes you planned are never recorded as events, because they haven't happened yet.</li>
            <li>Drafting has its own monthly budget, $10 unless you set another in the world's settings, apart from the daily budget that pays for extraction and Ask. The Prep page shows what this month has spent.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>October 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Nested lists survive. Reopening a saved note used to flatten indented bullets, and a numbered list's sub-items came back as a separate list. The editor now reads notes with the same parser the rest of the app uses, and saves lists at the depth you wrote them.</li>
            <li>A deploy no longer signs everyone out. Sign-ins used to be lost, and any form left open failed, every time a new version went out.</li>
            <li>Only a GM can file a GM note. The capture page already kept the choice from players; now the server refuses one too.</li>
            <li>Opening nornis.app without an account shows the welcome page instead of going straight to the sign-in screen. Members still land on their dashboard.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 28, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The Loremaster answers as it writes. Ask a question — on the Ask page, in the panel beside your notes, or on a public world — and the words appear as they come instead of all at once after a wait; how sure the answer is shows as soon as the record has been searched, and the sources it cites link up when it finishes. If the answer is cut off partway, you are told so rather than left with half of one. Leaving mid-answer, or switching worlds, stops it there.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 17, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The notes editor can take the whole screen, on the capture page and when editing a source's body. A maximize button at the far end of the formatting toolbar grows the editor to the viewport with the reading measure centred and the toolbar pinned; the same button, or <kbd>Esc</kbd>, puts it back. Nothing typed is disturbed either way.</li>
            <li>Notes you are typing on the capture page are kept as you go, in your own browser, along with the title, type, visibility, date and campaign. Leave the page by accident — a sidebar click, a closed tab, a dropped connection — and the next visit puts everything back with a note saying how long ago it was kept and a <em>Start fresh</em> button beside it. Saving the source forgets the kept copy. Nothing is sent anywhere until you save.</li>
            <li>The same net now covers editing a source's body and a character's sheet, which it did not at first — a session's notes were lost on the edit page for exactly that reason. Leave mid-edit and the page says <em>You have unsaved changes</em> next to Edit, with <em>Resume editing</em> and <em>Discard</em>; open the editor and your changes are already in it. Save or Cancel forgets the kept copy; the character sheet editor also gained the maximize button.</li>
            <li>While you are typing in the notes editor, <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>←</kbd> and <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>→</kbd> no longer act as the browser's Back and Forward. They sit one key from the word-jump shortcuts, and Back from the middle of a page of notes is how notes get lost. Everywhere else in the app they still work as the browser intends.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 16, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The number beside <em>What you learned</em> is now exactly the number of entries on the page. The two were computed separately — the badge counted reveals, the page showed reveals and session records and dropped anything with nothing left to show — so they could disagree in both directions. The badge is now the page's own read, counted, and session records count towards it the way they appear on the page.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 13, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Deleting a world works again. A world mid-campaign remembers which campaign it is playing, and the wipe was removing the campaigns while the world still pointed at one, which the database refused. The pointer is let go first now, and the wipe also clears the tables added since it was written — shelf links, sheet snapshots, digests, import sessions and tutorial progress — rather than trusting the database to cascade them.</li>
            <li>Revealing a fact whose truth was <em>Hidden</em> now confirms it. Hidden is the truth only the GM may see, so a fact revealed to the party but left Hidden was invisible to them — What you learned counted the disclosure on the nav and then had nothing to show. Reveal confirms what it discloses, and a party-visible fact whose truth was still hidden can be revealed on its own.</li>
            <li>The <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> results read left to right instead of sitting centred.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 10, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The Codex is at <code>/codex</code>. Its pages were filed under <code>/artifacts</code>, the name the code uses for an entry; the address now says what the sidebar says. Old <code>/artifacts</code> links redirect.</li>
            <li>Addresses you can read. An entry, session, campaign, character or document now opens at its name — <code>/codex/the-ashen-king</code>, <code>/campaigns/ash-and-salt</code> — instead of a string of hex, on the public site as well as inside a world. The name in the address is fixed when the thing is first written and stays put through renames, so a link you have shared keeps meaning what it meant; two things with the same name get a number. Every old link still works, and opening one quietly rewrites the address to the readable form.</li>
            <li>The demo world's tutorial learned the new sidebar. Each step now says where it is done — <em>World › Map</em>, <em>Play › Review</em> — read from the navigation itself rather than written by hand, so it cannot drift again. Chapter one adds the campaign page and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>; chapter two's reveal leads to the Reveal page, and the closer is opening What you learned as a player to find the Castellan's design waiting there with your note beside it. Anyone partway through keeps every step they had done.</li>
            <li>The public face of a world caught up with the rest of it. A public entry, source and campaign now open on the same page shape members see — the header, the rule, the body beside a rail that counts what the page holds and links to what it connects to, with <em>Ask this world</em> in the corner where the GM has funded it. An entry says who plays it; a library excerpt says which document and pages it came from; and there is a Campaigns tab at last, with each run of play's introduction, the party's telling of the story so far, the cast and who plays them, what the campaign touched, and its sessions. The Sessions tab is called Sources now, since it carries journals and excerpts too. Reveal records — what a GM disclosed to the table — stay off the public site entirely, listed or by address.</li>
            <li>The review queue can be narrowed. Two pickers above the proposals filter by type — the chip on each card — and by the visibility a proposal would set. The pickers only offer what is actually waiting, and <em>Accept all</em> and <em>Reject all</em> act on what you can see, so filtering to GM-only facts and accepting all does exactly that.</li>
            <li>A shelf for the players who aren't on Nornis. On the Party page a GM can give anyone who plays without an account a <em>shelf link</em>: a private address that opens the world's party shelf — the books, maps and handouts on the Library's party side — with no login, nothing to agree to, and nothing else behind it. Copy it, or hold up the QR code across the table. Each person gets their own, you can see when they last opened it, and taking it back is one click; a new one replaces the old. It is a link to one person, not a publication: it isn't listed anywhere, search engines are told to leave it alone, and it never opens the GM's shelf.</li>
            <li>A public world's Sessions page now lists everything the party can see — journals and library excerpts as well as session notes — where before it showed only session notes and imported notes. The pages themselves were always reachable; the list now agrees with them.</li>
            <li>The stonemark, off the logo and onto the page. The rule with the diamond that sits under the N now sits under every entry's title and under the heading of each public page, and the branched rune above it — many sources feeding one trunk — marks the places where the record is still empty. Quiet ink, never the accent.</li>
            <li>The Library can feed the record now. On any codex entry a GM can open <em>Enrich from the library</em>: Nornis finds the passages in your indexed documents that look like they are about that entry, you tick the ones that belong, and they are filed as a source — one per document — and sent through extraction like a session note. The four pages of the Player's Guide about a city become proposals for that city's entry, reviewed like everything else, and its summary is rewritten from what you accept. A document's own page has the direct form of the same thing: name a page range, optionally an entry, and file it. Whole books are still never extracted; the pages you choose are the gate, an excerpt is capped in length, and it keeps the shelf the document was on.</li>
            <li>The party is who plays, not who logs in. A GM can add someone to the Party page by name — the friend who plays every week and would rather not use Nornis — and create their characters for them, with sheets and snapshots the GM keeps on their behalf. If they sign up later, one click on their settings page claims the player and everything they play becomes theirs; the GM can do it for them too. Leaving a world no longer takes your characters with you: the person stays at the table.</li>
            <li>Members is now only about accounts and roles. Everywhere a character is shown, it says who plays them by name.</li>
            <li>Giving someone a character is one field now, on Party for the GM and on Your settings for yourself: pick a character already at the table and it moves to them, pick a person the record knows from your sessions and a character is made from that entry, or type a name nobody has and it is created. Each choice says where it came from, and the GM can now move a character between players outright.</li>
            <li>A campaign is edited on its own page now — name, introduction, status, the dates it ran, its cast, and, at the bottom, its removal — the way everything else here is edited where it is read. The Campaigns list keeps what only makes sense over the whole list: the order, and adding a new one. Dates can be cleared again, which they could not be before.</li>
            <li>A campaign's dates now do some work. Give a campaign the dates it ran and its page offers the GM every source that falls inside them and is filed under no campaign — a cleanup for the sessions captured before the campaign existed, or imported without one. Review the list, untick the odd night that belonged to a side game, and file the rest in one click. Nornis only ever offers; it never moves a source on its own, and never one already filed somewhere else.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>Nornis 2.0 — September 10, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>A new surface. The navy sidebar and the gold are gone; Nornis is paper now — one warm ground, ink for text, a sidebar that is a tint of the page rather than a block beside it, and one accent, a terracotta, kept for the button that does the thing and the icon of where you are. Titles and the record's own text are set in Newsreader, the chrome in IBM Plex Sans. Gold stays on the stonemark, where it belongs.</li>
            <li>Dark, if you want it. Your settings has an Appearance section: light, dark, or match this device. The choice is kept on the device you make it on, so a phone read in bed and a laptop at the table can each have their own.</li>
            <li>Every entry, session, campaign, character and document now opens on the same page shape: the title and its facts across the top, the reading down the left, and a rail on the right that says what the thing connects to and how much the record holds on it, with the Loremaster in the same corner on every page. The rail only ever counts what the page itself was allowed to load, so a player's rail and a GM's rail can differ in number but never in kind.</li>
            <li>Find your way. The sidebar is grouped by what you're doing — Play, World, Table, and for GMs a fourth — instead of one long list, and every page now says where it sits in your world in a breadcrumb up top.</li>
            <li>Jump to anything: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (or the search box under the world name), type a few letters, and go straight to a campaign, a character, an entry, a session, or a document. It remembers what you opened last.</li>
            <li>Campaigns, Party, and Members each have a page of their own that everyone at the table can read. Campaigns lists the one you're playing first; Party shows every member's characters with a link to each; Members moved out of the GM's settings, where only the GM could see who was at the table.</li>
            <li>The vault importer has a door from Capture, and the campaign a session belongs to is a link wherever it appears.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 7–9, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Every character has a page. Open one to see what the record knows about the person you play — what they carry, where they've been, who they answer to, what they're caught up in — grouped for reading about someone rather than as one long list.</li>
            <li>A sheet of your own on that page: free-form, in Markdown, for tables whose real sheet is on paper. Nornis stores and shows it and never computes with it — no parsing, no validation, no field whose unit it claims to understand. It is yours and the GM's until you share it with the party.</li>
            <li>Snapshots of the paper sheet: attach a photograph or scan you've already captured, dated for when the sheet was current, and keep as many as you like, newest first. Detaching one keeps the source.</li>
            <li>Under the sheet, a quiet note of items the record connects to your character that the sheet doesn't mention — an observation, not a correction, and it never edits the sheet.</li>
            <li>The cast on a campaign page links to each character's page, so you can reach anyone in the run, not just the characters you play.</li>
            <li>Under the hood: the services now reach the database, file storage, and the queue as themselves rather than with stored keys, and hosting was trimmed — images moved to GitHub's registry and the probes stopped paying for telemetry.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>August 7–24, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>What to reveal next: the reveal page now opens on the hidden material whose moment has come — the truth under a storyline the party keeps circling, the fact that contradicts what they currently believe, the reveal that costs nothing to make — ranked, each with its reasons.</li>
            <li>What you learned: players get a page of everything the GM has disclosed since they last looked, with the GM's note beside each item and a count on the nav until they've seen it.</li>
            <li>Campaigns have a page of their own: the cast, the places, and the record, assembled from what the campaign's sessions cite. A world now names the campaign it is playing, so new session notes file into the right one instead of none.</li>
            <li>The landing page is a dashboard titled with the world you're in, and the navigation follows the order the work actually runs in.</li>
            <li>Handwritten pages are read before extraction, not during it: the capture page transcribes them, shows you the text, and waits for your say-so, so a misread name never becomes canon nobody typed. On a phone the camera opens directly — and the button now works on iOS.</li>
            <li>Accepting a proposal that names an entry the batch doesn't create no longer dead-ends. Nornis resolves it — to an existing entry when the match is exact, or by proposing the missing one — and stops to ask when the call is the GM's.</li>
            <li>Fixed: menus that stopped responding to clicks, and the world digest's typography and spacing.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>August 2–6, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>World digest: a short read on where the world stands, on the dashboard. GMs get the full record; the party's version is written from what the party can see.</li>
            <li>Ask answers can be filed back into the record. An answer worth keeping becomes a GM note that runs through extraction like any other source, so what it established turns into facts you review.</li>
            <li>Entry summaries now refresh themselves when accepted changes land, instead of describing the world as it was when the summary was written.</li>
            <li>Continuity Health finds duplicate entries — the same person, place, or thing recorded twice — and drafts the merge, naming which one survives and why. It only raises a pair it is sure about.</li>
            <li>A source stuck mid-processing now gets a way out an hour after it wedges, instead of staying stuck until someone noticed.</li>
            <li>Merging, revealing, or wrapping up no longer leaves an entry's summary describing the world before the change.</li>
            <li>Drafting a fix for the same continuity finding twice no longer fills the review queue with duplicate copies of the same proposals.</li>
            <li>Under the hood: paid AI can be switched off without a redeploy, and the test and deploy pipeline was rebuilt to run once per push instead of twice.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>July 21 – August 1, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>A status page at <a href="https://status.nornis.app">status.nornis.app</a>, linked from the footer. It reports whether Nornis and the services it depends on are healthy, and it is hosted outside the system it watches — so when something is wrong, the page telling you still loads.</li>
            <li>Tightened visibility in several places where private or GM-only material could surface to someone who should not see it: the review queue, the session feed on Ask, and edits to facts and relationships. Nothing about what you had marked private changed — where it was honoured did.</li>
            <li>Extraction failures no longer disappear quietly. A job that fails for good is now noticed rather than lost, and there are tools to look at it and send it back through.</li>
            <li>Fast drawing on the ink canvas no longer occasionally creates two session notes, one of them orphaned without your strokes.</li>
            <li>Revealing a fact and correcting it in the same action no longer fails. The same applied to accepting two proposals that touched one artifact — the second would error out every time, not occasionally.</li>
            <li>Starting a replay twice in quick succession can no longer leave two running against one world, with cancel and advance each finding an arbitrary one.</li>
            <li>Emptying a session note's body or link now actually empties it. Clearing the editor and saving reported success but quietly kept the old text — and never triggered reprocessing, so the record stayed built on words no longer there.</li>
            <li>Pages no longer paint the wrong thing when you move faster than the network. Clicking through the codex, switching worlds while a page loads, changing the campaign filter, or adjusting the cost date range could each leave the slower reply to land last and win — showing an artifact you had already navigated past, another world's continuity score, or costs for a period the picker no longer showed. Every one of those now discards a reply that arrived for something you have left.</li>
            <li>Merging two artifacts that were already related to each other no longer leaves a stale link behind. Previously the merged-away duplicate stayed listed as a connection on the survivor, and Continuity Health raised it as an unresolvable finding after every such merge — one that no amount of tidying could clear.</li>
            <li>Under the hood: deploys now check the app actually came up before reporting success, and the background worker reports in so a stall is visible instead of silent. Plus a long housekeeping pass — duplicated logic folded together, dead scaffolding removed.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="nornis-changelog-entry">
        <h2>July 16–20, 2026</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Public world sharing: give a world a public address and switch it on — anyone gets a read-only view of party-visible material only (the codex, the timeline, and session notes), as an anonymous observer. Nothing private or GM-only ever appears, and you can switch it off again at any time.</li>
            <li>Library: keep the sourcebooks, maps, and handouts your table actually references — immutable, indexed PDFs that answer through the Loremaster with page citations, and never become canon.</li>
